>
  <p>
    The PS Vita was a brilliant handheld that Sony abandoned too early, and its library is
    the reason it still has a devoted following today. From the gravity-defying
    <strong>Gravity Rush</strong> to the genre-defining RPG <strong>Persona 4 Golden</strong>,
    the Vita collected some of the best portable games ever made — plus a wave of indie
    titles that felt perfectly at home on its OLED screen. Here is our ranked list of the
    25 best PS Vita games, along with how to actually buy and play them in 2026.
  </p>

  <h2>The state of the PS Vita in 2026</h2>

  <p>
    First, some context, because buying Vita games today is different from most consoles.
    Sony stopped making the handheld in 2019, and while the digital storefront is still
    open, it is not what it used to be — you can no longer pay by card directly, so you
    have to top up your PSN wallet from a PS4, PS5, or the website first. Online
    multiplayer is gone for most titles. That leaves two realistic routes: buy digital
    while the store survives, or hunt down physical cartridges on the second-hand market,
    where common games stay cheap and rare ones climb fast.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-figure">
    <img
      src="/images/ps-vita-top5.svg"
      alt="The top five PS Vita games: Gravity Rush, Persona 4 Golden, Tearaway, Hotline Miami, TxK"
      loading="lazy"
      width="800"
      height="450"
    />
    <figcaption>Gravity Rush and Persona 4 Golden top the list, with Tearaway, Hotline Miami, and TxK close behind.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The top 5 PS Vita games</h2>

  <h3>1. Gravity Rush</h3>
  <p>
    Gravity Rush remains the best showcase of what the Vita could do. As the amnesiac Kat,
    you manipulate gravity to fall sideways, walk on walls, and soar through a beautifully
    stylized floating city. The motion controls and touchscreen are used in ways that
    actually add to the experience rather than feeling bolted on, and it remains one of
    the most imaginative action games Sony has published. It later moved to PS4, but the
    Vita original is still a landmark.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Persona 4 Golden</h3>
  <p>
    The definitive version of one of the best JRPGs ever made, Persona 4 Golden alone
    justified buying a Vita for many people. It blends a turn-based dungeon crawler with a
    high-school life sim, and the added content over the PS2 original makes it the version
    to play. It has since been ported to PC and consoles, but for years the Vita was the
    only way to play it — and it is still a perfect fit for a handheld.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Tearaway</h3>
  <p>
    Media Molecule built Tearaway around the Vita&apos;s hardware quirks — the rear touch
    panel, the camera, the microphone — and turned them into a papercraft platformer that
    is as charming as it is inventive. It is the rare game where the gimmicks are the whole
    point and they all work. A PS4 version followed, but the Vita original is the purest
    expression of the idea.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Hotline Miami</h3>
  <p>
    The Vita version of Hotline Miami is widely considered the best way to play it. The
    fast, brutal, one-hit-death loop fits handheld play perfectly, and the OLED screen
    makes its neon-soaked visuals pop. It is one of several indie titles that felt like
    they were made for the hardware, and it still holds up as one of the best action games
    of its era.
  </p>

  <h3>5. TxK</h3>
  <p>
    Jeff Minter&apos;s TxK is a modern take on the classic tube shooter, distilled to pure
    arcade bliss. You zip around the edges of geometric shapes blasting everything in your
    path, backed by a hypnotic soundtrack and Minter&apos;s signature psychedelic visuals.
    It is a cult favorite for a reason — and because it never got a wide physical release,
    the digital version is the one to grab before it becomes even harder to find.
  </p>

  <h2>The rest of the best (6–25)</h2>

  <ul class="game-list">
    <li><strong>6. Severed</strong> — DrinkBox&apos;s touch-based slash-&apos;em-up with a surprisingly strong story (2016).</li>
    <li><strong>7. Dragon&apos;s Crown</strong> — Vanillaware&apos;s gorgeous modern brawler with deep RPG systems (2013).</li>
    <li><strong>8. Danganronpa: Trigger Happy Havoc</strong> — The definitive Vita murder-mystery visual novel (2013).</li>
    <li><strong>9. Spelunky</strong> — The best roguelike platformer on the handheld (2013).</li>
    <li><strong>10. Uncharted: Golden Abyss</strong> — A full Uncharted adventure crammed into the launch lineup (2011).</li>
    <li><strong>11. Guacamelee!</strong> — A luchador-themed Metroidvania with real personality (2013).</li>
    <li><strong>12. Odin Sphere Leifthrasir</strong> — Vanillaware&apos;s dramatically improved PS2 remake (2016).</li>
    <li><strong>13. Velocity 2X</strong> — A frantic shoot-and-teleport hybrid with a rare, pricey cartridge (2014).</li>
    <li><strong>14. Ys: Memories of Celceta</strong> — A fast-paced action RPG remake (2013).</li>
    <li><strong>15. Zero Escape: Virtue&apos;s Last Reward</strong> — A mind-bending puzzle-mystery visual novel (2012).</li>
    <li><strong>16. Rogue Legacy</strong> — Generational roguelike where every death passes traits to a descendant (2014).</li>
    <li><strong>17. The Walking Dead: Season One</strong> — Telltale&apos;s choice-driven classic, perfect on handheld (2013).</li>
    <li><strong>18. Dragon Quest Builders</strong> — Minecraft with structure and a Dragon Quest skin (2016).</li>
    <li><strong>19. Lumines: Electronic Symphony</strong> — A mesmerizing rhythm-puzzle fusion for the OLED (2012).</li>
    <li><strong>20. Mortal Kombat</strong> — A staggering amount of fighting content in portable form (2012).</li>
    <li><strong>21. Dariusburst Chronicle Saviours</strong> — A colossal shmup with hundreds of stages (2015).</li>
    <li><strong>22. WipEout 2048</strong> — The best racing game on the system, launch-lineup gold (2012).</li>
    <li><strong>23. Soul Sacrifice Delta</strong> — An ambitious Monster Hunter alternative with a dark twist (2014).</li>
    <li><strong>24. Minecraft: PlayStation Vita Edition</strong> — The portable world-builder that just works (2014).</li>
    <li><strong>25. Killzone: Mercenary</strong> — The Vita&apos;s best first-person shooter (2013).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If you only play a few, start with these genres</h2>

  <p>
    The full list spans a lot of ground, so here is a quicker way in based on what you
    actually like. For <strong>RPGs</strong>, Persona 4 Golden and Dragon Quest Builders are
    the essentials, with Ys: Memories of Celceta for action fans. For <strong>action and
    platforming</strong>, Gravity Rush, Uncharted: Golden Abyss, and Guacamelee! are the
    standouts. For <strong>indie and arcade</strong>, Hotline Miami, Spelunky, TxK, and
    Velocity 2X are where the Vita&apos;s OLED really shines. And for <strong>visual novels and
    puzzles</strong>, the Danganronpa and Zero Escape series are the genre&apos;s best work on
    the system.
  </p>

  <h2>How to buy PS Vita games today</h2>

  <p>
    Your two options are digital and physical, and they come with different trade-offs.
    The digital store is still live and tends to be the cheapest way to play, but you
    cannot pay by card directly anymore — add funds to your PSN wallet from a PS4, PS5, or
    the PlayStation website first. Physical cartridges are plentiful for common games and
    easy to find second-hand, but the rare titles with tiny print runs — Velocity 2X, TxK,
    and various Limited Run releases — now command serious collector prices.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-figure">
    <img
      src="/images/ps-vita-buy.svg"
      alt="Comparison of buying PS Vita games digitally versus physical cartridges"
      loading="lazy"
      width="800"
      height="450"
    />
    <figcaption>Digital is cheapest but requires wallet top-ups; physical cartridges are easier for common games but pricey for rare ones.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    If you just want to play, buy digital while the store is still available. If you want
    a physical collection, start with the common titles and be prepared to pay a premium
    for the cult favorites. Either way, the Vita&apos;s library is deep enough that it is
    still worth the effort in 2026.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>

  <p>
    The PS Vita may be discontinued, but its best games are timeless. Gravity Rush, Persona
    4 Golden, and Tearaway are worth the price of entry on their own, and the deep bench of
    indie and JRPG titles means you will never run out of things to play. Grab the digital
    versions of the essentials while the store is still open, and chase the physical
    cartridges you care about. It is one of the few handhelds where the library has aged
    better than the hardware.
  </p>
